-- Variadic builtins (`fmt`, `fmt2`) inside list literals.
--
-- For fixed-arity builtins like `str` and `at`, list-literal parsing
-- auto-expands the call (see `listlit-fnref-greedy.ilo`). For variadic
-- builtins like `fmt` and `fmt2`, the parser can't know how many of the
-- following tokens are call args, so the bare form `[k fmt2 rv 2]` would
-- silently fall through as four list elements and the verifier would
-- emit a misleading "undefined variable 'fmt2'".
--
-- ILO-P101 catches this at parse time and tells the agent to use one of
-- the two ergonomic shapes below.

-- Workaround A: wrap the call in parens.
-- Each set of parens groups the call as one list element.
row-parens k:t rv:n>L t;[k (fmt2 rv 2)]

-- Workaround B: bind first, then list the binding.
-- Use this when the same value is needed in more than one place, or
-- when the inline form gets unreadable.
row-bind k:t rv:n>L t;s=fmt2 rv 2;[k s]

-- A typical CSV row mixes both: known-arity calls auto-expand, variadic
-- ones get parens.
csv-row k:t c:t rv:n>L t;[k c (fmt2 rv 2)]

-- run: row-parens "foo" 3.14
-- out: [foo, 3.14]
-- run: row-bind "foo" 3.14
-- out: [foo, 3.14]
-- run: csv-row "x" "y" 2.5
-- out: [x, y, 2.50]
